
The password for the next level (Bandit 2) is stored in a file called - (a single hyphen/dash) located right in the home directory.


The Problem
If you try to read it normally using cat -, the command will hang or just mimic what you type. In Linux, the - character is a special symbol that tells a command to read from Standard Input (stdin) instead of looking for a physical file name. 

------------------------------

Step-by-Step Solution## Step 1: Log into Bandit Level 1
If you haven't already, log into Level 1 using the password you found in Level 0. Open your terminal and run:

ssh bandit1@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

(Paste the password from Level 0 when prompted).


Step 2: Verify the File Exists
List the files in your current directory to verify the hyphen file is there:

ls

You will see a file simply named -.


Step 3: Read the Dash File
To bypass the special meaning of the hyphen, you must explicitly specify the file path. By adding ./ (which means "current directory"), you force Linux to treat it as a regular file name.
Run this command:

cat ./-



Step 4: Grab the Password & Exit
The terminal will print out an alphanumeric string (e.g., CV1DtqXWVFXTvM2F0k09SHz0YwRINYA9). Copy this string—it is your password for Level 2!
Type exit to close the current session.


------------------------------


## Moving to Level 2
Log back in as the next user using your newly acquired password:

ssh bandit2@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

